  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: We're continuing to watch a slowly northeastward \nadvancing mid-lvl cloud deck early this afternoon. This cloud layer \nmarks the advancing edge of moisture (and instability) advecting \nwithin the 850:700 mb layer. A parcel lifted from this level still \nappears to be fighting a bit of inhibition, which will keep only \nvery low PoPs in the forecast as this moves northeast.  Elsewhere, \nwildfire smoke aloft remains quite thick, and may have slight \nimpacts on high temperatures this afternoon. \n\nTONIGHT: The forecast remains somewhat uncertain this evening and \novernight, though there are at least better signals for where \nconvection may form after dark today. Latest short term guidance \npoints to two distinct areas of focus. One near the SD/ND border \ninto west central Minnesota where current AC field ends up by \nsunset.   The initiation of at least a 25 to 35 knot LLJ should be \nenough to develop a few storms.  It's possible that a minor \nshortwave could also drop southeast after midnight helping push this \nactivity towards Highway 14 and SW Minnesota by daybreak. A second \narea to watch will be northwest and north central Iowa where \nslightly stronger LLJ convergence develops. Any activity that forms \nshould pass along the far eastern CWA by 12Z.  Realistic MUCAPE \nbetween 1500-2000 J/KG with 20-25 knots of shear may produce a few \nstronger storms with large hail. \n\nSUNDAY: A continued uncertain forecast remains for Sunday. Morning \nconvection should weaken by mid-morning as the LLJ weakens and any \nactivity pushes east of the CWA.  In it's wake, there remains no \nstrong triggering mechanisms for any convection further west and low-\nlvl and surface flow may turn more northeasterly by mid-day. \nNevertheless while we may see CAPE values push 2500-3000 J/KG, HREF \nprobabilities of less than 100 J/KG CIN remain only around 25%, \nsuggesting that surface based convection may be hard to come by. \nWildfire smoke will again be quite thick on Sunday, and have lowered \ntemperatures a degree or two. Though the ongoing drought may also \ncounter act some of those limiting effects.  \n\nSUNDAY NIGHT: Slightly increased PoPs arrive late Sunday evening \ninto Monday as there seems to be some mid-lvl vorticity moving \nthrough the region. While highly uncertain on the track, this could \nat least pose some risks for stronger convection forming early in \nthe evening in western SD/ND on the nose of an increasing LLJ. This \nconvection will be initially surface based and capable of very \nlarge hail. There are some hints that these storms could congeal\nand begin to produce a bit more wind as they reach central SD. \nThe limiting factor in the eastward progression could be a very\ndry sub 600 mb layer as these storms reach the James River \nvalley. \n\nMONDAY-WEDNESDAY: The first half of the week continues to look very \nuncertain given the quasi-zonal or slightly northwest flow pattern \naloft.  It's certainly possible one or more of these days will \nfeature a rain chance, but these are all dependent on any weak eddy \nin the flow. There continues to be some consistency again today on \none of these waves arriving Monday evening into Tuesday and again \ninto Wednesday morning. The available instability would suggest\nat least a continued low-risk for a severe storm or two, though\nwind shear continues to be borderline. Temperatures remain near\ntheir seasonal normals in the lower to middle 80s. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: No changes in this portion of the forecast, \nwhere mid-lvl flow flattens and becomes westerly. PoPs will be \nelevated into Thursday as a cold front may pass through the \nregion. There continues to be growing signs of a slight cooldown\ninto the weekend.",
